30. IMPORTANT NOTES & COMMONLY CONFUSED CONCEPTS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

I. Axis Numbering
                 — First axis = axis=0 | Second axis = axis=1 | Third axis = axis=2
                 — Axis numbers always start from 0.

II. Reduction Functions
                       — sum(), mean(), min(), max(), std(), var(), any(), all(), argmax(), argmin()
                       — axis=0 → (column-wise result) | axis=1 → (row-wise result).

III. Selection / Modification Functions
                                       — delete(), insert(), take()
                                       — axis=0 → rows | axis=1 → columns.

IV. Broadcasting Rule
                     — Dimensions are compared from the last axis.
                     — Dimensions must be equal or one of them must be 1.

V. reshape() vs resize()
                        — reshape() → returns reshaped array without changing data size.
                        — resize() → can repeat/truncate data to fit new size.

VI. flatten() vs ravel()
                        — flatten() → always returns a copy.
                        — ravel() → returns a view whenever possible.

VII. transpose() vs T
                      — arr.T → shorthand transpose.
                      — np.transpose() → can reorder any axes.

VIII. View vs Copy
                  — View → shares memory with original array.
                  — Copy → independent memory.

IX. np.where()
               — np.where(condition) → returns indices.
               — np.where(condition, x, y) → returns selected values.

X. any() vs all()
                 — any() → at least one True.
                 — all() → every value must be True.

XI. argmax() / argmin()
                       — Return indices, not values.

XII. argsort() vs sort()
                        — sort() → sorted values.
                        — argsort() → indices that sort the array.

XIII. partition() vs argpartition()
                                   — partition() → partially sorted values.
                                   — argpartition() → indices of partial sort.

XIV. diag() vs diagonal()
                         — diag() → create or extract diagonal.
                         — diagonal() → only extracts diagonal.

XV. eye() vs identity()
                        — eye() → rectangular matrix and diagonal offset supported.
                        — identity() → square identity matrix only.

XVI. repeat() vs tile()
                        — repeat() → repeats individual elements.
                        — tile() → repeats the whole array.

XVII. concatenate() vs stack()
                              — concatenate() → joins along an existing axis.
                              — stack() → creates a new axis.

XVIII. column_stack() vs hstack()
                                 — column_stack() → converts 1D arrays into columns.
                                 — hstack() → simply joins horizontally.

XIX. split() vs hsplit() vs vsplit()
                                    — split() → any axis.
                                    — hsplit() → columns.
                                    — vsplit() → rows.

XX. C Order vs Fortran Order
                            — C → row-major.
                            — F → column-major.

XXI. nditer(order='C'/'F')
                           — Changes iteration order only.
                           — Does NOT transpose the array.

XXII. nditer(flags=['external_loop'])
                                     — Returns chunks instead of single elements.

XXIII. None vs np.newaxis
                          — Both are exactly equivalent.

XXIV. keepdims=True
                    — Keeps reduced dimensions as size 1.
                    — Useful for broadcasting.

XXV. np.average()
                  — Supports weights.
                  — np.mean() does not.

XXVI. Covariance vs Correlation
                               — Covariance → do variables move together?
                               — Correlation → how strongly do they move together? (-1 to 1)

XXVII. Variance vs Standard Deviation
                                     — Variance = SD².
                                     — Standard deviation has the same units as the data.

XXVIII. Fancy Indexing vs Boolean Indexing
                                         — Fancy → integer indices.
                                         — Boolean → True/False mask.

XXIX. np.array_equal() vs np.equal()
                                    — array_equal() → one True/False for whole arrays.
                                    — equal() → element-wise comparison.

XXX. np.isin()
               — Preferred over deprecated np.in1d().

XXXI. np.nan Functions
                      — nanmean(), nansum(), nanmin(), nanmax()
                      — Ignore NaN values during computation.

XXXII. Scientific Notation
                          — np.set_printoptions(suppress=True)
                          — Prevents scientific notation for small floats.

XXXIII. threshold=sys.maxsize
                             — Prints the complete array without truncation.

XXXIV. edgeitems=n
                  — Controls how many edge elements are shown when arrays are truncated.

XXXV. item()
             — Converts one NumPy scalar into a native Python type.

XXXVI. tolist()
               — Converts an entire NumPy array into Python lists.

XXXVII. array.size vs nbytes
                            — size → total number of elements.
                            — nbytes → total memory occupied (bytes).

XXXVIII. itemsize
                 — Memory used by one element (bytes).

XXXIX. meshgrid()
                 — Used to create coordinate grids for vectorized computation.

XL. Structured Arrays
                     — Allow different dtypes in different named fields.

XLI. np.linalg.norm()
                    — Computes Euclidean length by default.

XLII. np.count_nonzero()
                       — Counts all non-zero elements efficiently.

XLIII. np.bincount()
                    — Works only with non-negative integer arrays.

XLIV. np.unpackbits()
                     — Converts uint8 integers into binary bits.

XLV. Image ↔ NumPy
                  — np.array(Image) → Image to array.
                  — Image.fromarray(array) → Array to image.
                  

